#!/usr/bin/env sh
# ^ This first line is called a "shebang" (or "hashbang"). When a file is
# marked executable and launched directly (e.g. `./docker-setup.sample`),
# the operating system reads this exact line to decide which program
# should interpret everything below it. `#!` marks it as a shebang rather
# than an ordinary comment; `/usr/bin/env` is a small helper program that
# searches your PATH for whatever command name you give it; `sh` is the
# POSIX shell (a minimal, universally-available command interpreter). So
# together this line means "run the rest of this file as a shell script,
# using whichever `sh` is found on this machine."
#
# --- What is this file? (context for every file in .kamal/hooks/) ---
# This app is deployed with Kamal (kamal-deploy.org): a tool that builds a
# Rails app into a Docker container image and ships it to remote servers
# over SSH, without needing Heroku or Kubernetes. A deploy moves through a
# fixed sequence of stages (build the image, connect to hosts, set up
# Docker, boot the new app container, reboot the traffic proxy, and so
# on). At specific points in that sequence, Kamal will run a "hook" — an
# executable script living in this .kamal/hooks/ directory and named after
# the stage it belongs to — if one exists.
#
# This file is the "docker-setup" hook. Kamal runs it once per target
# server, right after confirming Docker itself is installed and ready on
# that server, but before your app's own container is built or started.
#
# The ".sample" suffix on the filename means Kamal will NOT run this file
# as-is. Kamal only executes a hook when a file with the exact stage name
# exists (here that would be "docker-setup", with no ".sample") and is
# marked executable. This file is only a template/example showing what
# such a hook could contain and which environment variables Kamal makes
# available to it. To activate it for real, copy it to "docker-setup"
# (dropping ".sample"), edit as desired, and make sure it's executable.

# A blank line here — it has no effect on the script; it's just whitespace
# separating the explanatory comments above from the code that runs below.

# `echo` prints whatever text follows it to standard output — in this
# context, that output shows up in Kamal's own deploy log in your
# terminal. The text is wrapped in double quotes, which allows shell
# variables written inside it to be "interpolated" (substituted with their
# current values) rather than printed literally.
#
# `$KAMAL_HOSTS` reads the environment variable named KAMAL_HOSTS. Kamal
# sets this variable before invoking the hook; it holds a comma-separated
# list of the server addresses/hostnames this deploy is targeting. So
# running this hook would print something like:
# "Docker set up on 1.2.3.4,5.6.7.8..."
echo "Docker set up on $KAMAL_HOSTS..."
